Apple has hired former <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-glasses/">Meta</a> lawyer Scott Tucker to lead its litigation team, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-16/apple-names-meta-veteran-as-litigation-chief-in-legal-team-shake-up">Mark Gurman</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2022/05/apple-park-at-night-1.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-844497" /><br/>
Tucker will become Apple's vice president of global litigation and financial services, a new position overseeing commercial lawsuits, patent disputes, and legal matters involving its financial products. He previously led litigation at &zwnj;Meta&zwnj; and held a senior legal role at Morgan Stanley.<br/>
<br/>
The move brings Tucker back together with Jennifer Newstead, Apple's general counsel. Newstead <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/07/apple-adds-three-executives-to-leadership-page/">joined Apple's leadership</a> in March after six years as &zwnj;Meta&zwnj;'s top lawyer.<br/>
<br/>
Heather Grenier, who currently oversees Apple's commercial and competition litigation, will report to Tucker instead of Newstead. She will remain on Newstead's leadership team. Apple is also expanding legal executive Kyle Andeer's responsibilities to include international legal matters, child safety, cybersecurity, and law enforcement.<br/>
<br/>
The changes are part of a wider overhaul under Newstead, who now oversees both legal and government affairs. Apple announced plans to combine the teams alongside the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/12/04/two-more-apple-executives-retiring/">retirements of Kate Adams and Lisa Jackson</a>.<br/>
<br/>
According to Gurman, some Apple executives believe the company should have performed better in court, particularly in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/app-store/">App Store</a> disputes. Its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/12/11/apple-app-store-fees-external-payment-links/">battle with Epic Games</a> has forced changes to the rules for links to outside payment options and the fees Apple can charge.<br/>
<br/>
